<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-210. THE ARRANGER'S CONTEXT MENU OFFERS NO ITEM THAT DOES NOTHING.

  It offered one. "Delete sample" was enabled and clickable and its handler
  was, in full:

      void SStdMixerView::ctDeleteSample()
      {
      }

  No edit, no message, not even a log line. The reasonable reading of a dead
  menu item is that deletion is broken, not that this one item was never
  finished.

  MEASURED, not assumed, while removing it: of the fifteen ct* slots this menu
  wires plus its lambda-connected items, ctDeleteSample was the ONLY empty
  body. ctCleanupTakes, ctCleanupTakesTracks and ctAddTrack are one statement
  each and all three delegate to a real worker.

  WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL NOW. Nothing could build this popup off screen
  before QBX-207, so no gate could have said "this menu offers an item that
  does nothing" about any of the twenty items on it.

  RETIRED RATHER THAN REPOINTED, and section (a) pins that CHOICE rather than
  just the absence of a dead handler. QBX-197 concluded the opposite for
  "Cleanup..." and the difference is the reason that rule rests on: there the
  entry point was the only discoverable answer to the user's question, so it
  was kept and renamed. Here the answer is "Remove sample", one line above it
  in the same menu, bound to Delete and widened to MIDI clips by QBX-202.
  "Remove" versus "Delete" is not a distinction this model makes; there is one
  removal verb. Section (c) is what makes that claim checkable instead of
  asserted: the surviving item really removes a clip, so nothing became less
  reachable. Should a later ticket decide a real Delete/Remove distinction
  exists, (a) is the assertion to change deliberately.

  NOT GATED HERE, deliberately:

    - the ITEM COUNT. `items=<n>` would catch any future do-nothing item
      generically, but it also fails on every legitimate addition to a menu
      that is still growing, so it would be noise rather than a gate. What
      replaces it is the audit above, recorded here because a future reader
      can repeat it in one command and cannot recover it from the diff.
    - the empty handler as a CLASS. There is no seam that can ask a QAction
      whether its slot does anything, so "offers no item that does nothing"
      is gated one named item at a time. This is that one.

  SECTION (c) TURNED OUT TO BE WORTH MORE THAN THE ABSENCE IT WAS WRITTEN FOR,
  and the measurement was a sabotage arm that failed to be noisy. S3 makes
  ctRemoveSample() return immediately; of the six cases run it failed exactly
  ONE, this one. delete_clip_in_group, comp_map_survives_duplicate and the rest
  all reach removal through the `remove-sample` VERB, which submits
  SRemoveSampleAction directly and never enters the slot.

  So ctRemoveSample() HAD NO GATE AT ALL: the slot behind both this menu item
  and the Delete/Backspace shortcut, which is the user's actual clip-deletion
  gesture. And the slot is where the interesting code is, not the action: the
  flat-index bug that made Delete do nothing on a grouped track, QBX-202's MIDI
  widening, and the autoUi_->deleteSelection() precedence rule all live there
  and nowhere else. Section (c) is the first gate on that gesture.

  Still ungated, and a separate claim from this one's: that precedence rule. A
  marquee of automation points is deleted BEFORE the clip, because a shortcut
  outranks a keyPressEvent, and only the SHORTCUT path can carry a marquee. No
  menu click reaches that branch, so this case structurally cannot.

  ONE NOTE FOR WHOEVER NAMES THESE ITEMS NEXT. Section (b) keys the surviving
  item by its LABEL, "Remove sample", because `actRemoveSample_` carries no
  objectName today and `sMenuKeyOf` falls back to the text. A ticket that gives
  it one moves that key, and this assertion has to move with it; the key is not
  a stable handle until the objectName exists.

  120 BPM, 4/4, 48 kHz. test_sawtooth.wav is 192000 frames.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="arranger_menu_no_dead_items">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <snap-to-grid-disable/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         (a) THE DEAD ITEM IS GONE, in BOTH shapes of this menu.

             It was built inside the `if( lastClickTrack_ )` block, not the
             clip block, so it was offered on a bare lane as well as over a
             clip and the absence has to be asserted in both: a right-click
             on empty lane space and a right-click on a clip build different
             menus from the same function.
         ================================================================= -->
    <assert-arranger-menu trackPath="0" at="1000"
                          absent="Delete sample"/>
    <assert-arranger-menu trackPath="0" at="1000" clip="0"
                          absent="Delete sample"/>

    <!-- ...and the menu refuses it as MISSING rather than offering it
         greyed. `arranger-menu` alone cannot tell those apart, which is why
         the pair above and below exists; this line is the half that says no
         script can reach a handler there any more. -->
    <arranger-menu trackPath="0" at="1000" clip="0" item="Delete sample"
                   expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         (b) THE SURVIVING ANSWER IS STILL OFFERED, AND ENABLED.

             This is the QBX-197 condition stated as an assertion: retiring
             a command is only safe while the question it answered still has
             a discoverable answer. If "Remove sample" were ever greyed or
             dropped here, removing "Delete sample" would have cost the user
             the gesture, and this line is what would say so.
         ================================================================= -->
    <assert-arranger-menu trackPath="0" at="1000" clip="0"
                          contains="Remove sample=1"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         (c) ...AND IT REALLY REMOVES THE CLIP.

             Offered and enabled is not the same as working, and an absence
             assertion on its own cannot distinguish "the duplicate was
             retired" from "the whole gesture was". This goes through the
             MENU, not through `remove-sample`, so what runs is the handler
             the retired item should have been pointing at all along.
         ================================================================= -->
    <arranger-menu trackPath="0" at="1000" clip="0" item="Remove sample"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- Undoable, like every other edit on this menu. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="192000"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
